# The national unit for china. Items take n=4; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g3-china-our-country-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=9 subject=history nation=china levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "China's first farmers"
  goal "Put the first farms and villages of China in order, and say how we know about farmers who left no writing."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=-7000 to=1000 step=1000 years=[-7000, -4800, -221] labels=["Rice farming in China", "Banpo village", "Qin unites China"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Rice was grown in the Yangtze valley by about 7000 BC. About 4800 BC, near today's Xi'an, the farmers of Banpo lived in round houses, grew millet, kept pigs and dogs, and made painted pots. Later the Shang kings ruled from about 1600 BC and wrote on bones. The Qin united China in 221 BC, and paper was improved in AD 105.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n3-what n=4
    }
    show history.n3-order n=4 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n3-between n=4 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n3-century n=4 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.across-year-one k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.which-century k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n3-order n=4 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n3-what n=4
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n3-century n=4 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.bc-ad-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n3-between n=4 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n3-why n=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n3-find-out n=4
  }
  remember {
    say "Every country's story goes back further than its writing. Where there is no writing, the ground and the buildings tell it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to put the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, evidence without
    writing) to work on the family's own country. The way in names what the place or thing was;
    the core orders three events of the country, counts the years between two, names a century,
    and reviews three of the grade's timeline questions; the two-star question asks how we know.
    The wrong idea to watch for is that a date in a book is when something began, when it is often
    only the first time it was written down or the oldest thing found. If the child is stuck, put
    the three events on a line you draw together. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you, near home. China's 道德与法治 in the middle grades asks children to learn about their homeland's long history and its people (recalled from the textbook series; the 2022 standard itself was not read), and Banpo's museum is one most children visit.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the place was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions stay on one side of year one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap in it, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
